# RADAR ↔ SONAR

> THE ECHO PAIR

![](/images/436.png)

RADAR asks the sky.

Cleanly.

A bright question
cast upward through blue air,
through weather,
through altitude,
through the thin white violence
of sunlit cloud.

It opens its invisible hand
over runways, towers, storms, wings.

Range.

Bearing.

Velocity.

Return.

The sky answers in angles.

It answers in sweep and blip,
in bright green arcs,
in weather bands,
in aircraft becoming numbers
against a clear and enormous field.

RADAR belongs to height.

To distance made crisp.
To threat made geometric.
To the white glare of atmosphere
where everything exposed
still insists it is free.

It asks:

what moves above us?

And the sky, offended but legible,
gives back its shapes.

SONAR asks the sea.

Not cleanly.

Nothing is clean
down there.

The question leaves the hull
and enters cold pressure.

It thickens.

It slows.

It travels through black water
where light has already failed,
where metal sweats,
where mud waits without language,
where wreckage becomes habitat,
where anything large enough
does not need to hurry.

SONAR does not receive the sky’s bright answer.

It receives delay.

A pulse goes out.

Then the dark considers it.

Then, from somewhere beyond sight,
something returns.

Not image.

Not proof.

A knock from the hidden world.

A shape made of waiting.

The sea answers in pressure.

In depth.
In distance measured by dread.
In the low round sound
of something unseen
agreeing, reluctantly,
to have existed.

RADAR is the clean question
thrown into open height.

SONAR is the heavy question
lowered into the closed dark.

One searches the bright above
where objects become targets
against air.

One searches the black below
where bodies become rumors
inside water.

But they are siblings.

Both send a pulse
into what refuses to confess.

Both strike the invisible
and listen for the bruise.

Both believe the unknown
has an obligation
to answer.

This is why they are dangerous.

Not because they detect.

Because they ask first.

Because they do not wait
for the world to speak voluntarily.

Because the sky may be vast,
and the sea may be sealed,
but both can be made
to return a sound.

RADAR asks:

where are you?

SONAR asks:

how deep?

And somewhere,
above the cloudline
or beneath the hull,

the hidden thing
answers

by becoming distance.